%[text] # Fit Polynomial to Data Using AI Agent
%[text] This example shows how to build an AI agent to fit polynomials up to ninth degree to data and choose the best fit.
%[text] This example uses: 
%[text] - The Agents SDK repo. \
%[text] - Curve Fitting Toolbox™ \
%[text] AI agents are programs that autonomously plan and execute workflows. Typically, agents use large language models (LLMs) to process user queries and identify actions that need to be taken, also known as *tool calls*. The agent executes the tool calls that the LLM has identified and returns the result to the LLM. Then, the LLM generates an answer or executes more tool calls.
%[text] The agent implemented in this example uses a ReAct architecture \[1\]. This architecture is an iterative workflow. At each step, the agent decides whether to reason in natural language or to invoke a tool call, until the agent generates a final answer. For an example of building a ReAct agent to solve a simple math problem, see [Solve Simple Math Problem Using AI Agent](https://github.com/matlab-deep-learning/llms-with-matlab/blob/main/examples/SolveSimpleMathProblemUsingAIAgent.md).
%[text] In this example, the agentic loop finds the best polynomial fit following these steps:
%[text] 1. Fit the data to polynomials with degrees 1 to *N* using the [`fit`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/curvefit/fit.html) function. The agent chooses *N* based on the user prompt. Save models and goodness-of-fit statistics.
%[text] 2. Evaluate the different fits. The agent has several tools to assess the fits. The agent chooses which assessment tools to call in what order.
%[text] 3. Choose the best fit. \
%[text] To make the workflow more robust, deterministic parts of the process, such as preprocessing data or plotting the final result, are not part of the agentic loop.
%[text] ![](text:image:8561)
%[text] ## Set Up Connection to OpenAI API
%[text] This example uses the OpenAI® API, which requires an OpenAI API key. For information on how to obtain an OpenAI API key, as well as pricing, terms and conditions of use, and information about available models, see the OpenAI documentation at [https://platform.openai.com/docs/overview](https://platform.openai.com/docs/overview).
%[text] To connect to the OpenAI API from MATLAB® using LLMs with MATLAB, specify the OpenAI API key as an environment variable and save it to a file called ".env".
%[text] ![](text:image:994c)
%[text] To connect to OpenAI, the ".env" file must be on the search path. Load the environment file using the [`loadenv`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/loadenv.html) function.
% loadenv(".env")
%%
%[text] ## Create Data
%[text] Generate data points. First, create a third-order polynomial. Then, add some random noise to the data. Finally, plot the data.
nData = 50;
x = linspace(-10,10,nData)';
a = 3;
b = -7;
c = 4;
d = 2;
y = a*(x).^3 + b*(x).^2 + c*x + d;
y = y + 100*rand(size(y));

figure %[output:28cc70e3]
plot(x,y,'.') %[output:28cc70e3]
%[text] Not all MATLAB datatypes can be easily expressed in a form that can be passed to or from an LLM (for example, complex numbers or polynomial fit datatypes). Large arrays may also degrade performance and increase cost. Instead of sending such raw data, keep it as internal state within the agent. To do this, store the values in a `workspace` structure that is passed into and updated by the functions defined below.
workspace.x = x;
workspace.y = y;
%%
%[text] ## Define Tools
%[text] To enable the LLM to make tool calls, use `LLMTool` to construct tools as understood by LLMs that bind to MATLAB funtions.
%[text] In this example, use an LLM to fit polynomials to data. Give the agent access to four tools:
%[text] - Fit polynomials of up to the highest potential degree to the data. Save the models and goodness-of-fit statistics.
%[text] - Analyze and assess the goodness-of-fit statistics.
%[text] - Analyze and assess the overfitting statistics. \
%[text] ### Fit Polynomials to Data
%[text] First, to keep track of the different potential polynomial fits, add information about potential polynomial degrees to the `workspace` structure using the `initializeCandidatePolynomialDegrees` function.
function workspace = initializeCandidatePolynomialDegrees(workspace,maxDegree)
    workspace.polynomials.Degree = 1:maxDegree;
    workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit = true(1,maxDegree);
end
%[text] Next, fit polynomials of each degree to the data and add the resulting models and goodness-of-fit statistics to the `workspace.polynomials` structure using the `computeCandidatePolynomials` function. 
%[text] To help the agent understand the function output, return a natural language description, `observation`, in addition to the updated `workspace` structure.
function [workspace,observation] = computeCandidatePolynomials(workspace)
polynomialDegrees = workspace.polynomials.Degree;
observation = "";
if any(polynomialDegrees >= 10)
    polynomialIsSupported = polynomialDegrees < 10;
    workspace.polynomials = workspace.polynomials(polynomialIsSupported,:);
    observation = "The fit function does not support polynomials with degrees greater than 9.";
end
for i = 1:numel(polynomialDegrees)
    fitType = "poly" + polynomialDegrees(i);
    
    [fittedModel,gof] = fit(workspace.x,workspace.y,fitType,Normalize="on");

    workspace.polynomials.FittedModel{i} = fittedModel;
    workspace.polynomials.GOF{i} = gof;
end
observation = observation + "Polynomials of up to degree " + max(polynomialDegrees) + " fitted successfully.";
end
%[text] The agent must call these two functions in order. Create a wrapper function called `fitPolynomialsToData` that first calls `initializeCandidatePolynomialDegrees` and then calls `computeCandidatePolynomials`.
function [observation, workspace] = fitPolynomialsToData(workspace,maxDegree)
workspace = initializeCandidatePolynomialDegrees(workspace,maxDegree);
[workspace,observation] = computeCandidatePolynomials(workspace);
end
%[text] Finally, create an `openAIFunction` object that represents the `fitPolynomialsToData` function. 
maxDegInput = aisdk.LLMToolArgument("maxDegree", ...
    "Description","Maximum polynomial degree", ...
    "Required", true, ...
    "DataType", "integer");
tools = aisdk.LLMTool(@fitPolynomialsToData, ...
    Description="Fit polynomials of up to a given maximum degree to data. Save the models and goodness-of-fit statistics.", ...
    InputArguments=maxDegInput, ...
    Workspace="agent");
%%
%[text] ### Reject Statistically Unstable Polynomial Fits
%[text] Use confidence bounds on coefficients to help you evaluate and compare fits. If the confidence bounds cross zero for a coefficient, this means you cannot be sure that it differs from zero. Therefore, reject fits with leading coefficients that have confidence bounds that cross zero.
%[text] The `rejectZeroCrossFits` function checks each polynomial fit to determine whether the confidence bounds on the highest order coefficient cross zero. If they do, then the function rejects the fit.
function [observation, workspace] = rejectZeroCrossFits(workspace)
polynomialDegrees = workspace.polynomials.Degree;
polynomialHasUnstableCoefficient = false(size(polynomialDegrees));
% Only analyze polynomials that have not already been rejected by other assessment tools
for i = polynomialDegrees(workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit)
    confidenceIntervals = confint(workspace.polynomials.FittedModel{i});
    polynomialHasUnstableCoefficient(i) = (confidenceIntervals(1,1) <= 0) & (confidenceIntervals(2,1) >= 0);
end
workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit = workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit & ~polynomialHasUnstableCoefficient;
log = sprintf("Rejected degrees %s. The confidence bounds of the leading coefficient crossed zero, indicating unstable fits.", ...
    jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(polynomialHasUnstableCoefficient)));
observation = "Remaining degrees: " + jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit)) + newline + "Log: " + log;
end
%[text] Create an `openAIFunction` object that represents the `rejectZeroCrossFits` function. To help the agent understand the purpose of the tool, provide a description.
tools(end+1) = aisdk.LLMTool(@rejectZeroCrossFits, ...
    Description="Assess the statistical stability of polynomial fits by determining whether the confidence bounds of the leading coefficients cross zero.", ...
    Workspace="agent");
%%
%[text] ### Inspect Plots of Polynomial Fits Using Vision Model
%[text] This is a simple sub-agent.
%[text] The `visuallyInspectPlotsOfPolynomialFits` function generates extrapolated plots of the polynomial fits and saves them to a temporary directory using the `createAndSaveExtrapolatedFitPlot` function, defined at the bottom of this example. The function then uses an LLM able to understand images, GPT-4.1 mini, to look at all of the plots and reject fits that look unreasonable. 
%[text] The function uses structured outputs to obtain a list of unrejected polynomial degrees from the LLM. For more information on structured outputs, see [Structured Output](file:../doc/OpenAI.md).
function [observation, workspace] = visuallyInspectPlotsOfPolynomialFits(workspace)
polynomialDegrees = workspace.polynomials.Degree;
% Create temporary directory in which to save fit plots.
tempDirectory = tempname(pwd);
mkdir(tempDirectory);
cleanupObj = onCleanup(@() rmdir(tempDirectory,"s"));

% Create and save plots, then add them to the message history.
msgContext = [];
for i = polynomialDegrees(workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit)
    imgName = createAndSaveExtrapolatedFitPlot(workspace,i,tempDirectory);
    msgContext = [msgContext, ...
        aisdk.LLMTextMessage("Polynomial degree " + i), ...
        aisdk.LLMImageMessage(imgName)]; %#ok<AGROW>
end

% Use vision model to assess the plots.
responseFormat = struct("rationale","Example rationale","isVisuallyReasonable",[1 2]);
client = aisdk.LLMClient("openai", "gpt-4.1-mini");
visionModel = aisdk.AIAgent(client, ...
    Messages=msgContext, ...
    Workspace=workspace, ...
    ResponseFormat=responseFormat);
try
    structResponse = run(visionModel, "Assess each polynomial fit plot. " + ...
        "Return the degrees that look visually reasonable.");
catch errMsg
    error("OpenAI request failed: %s", errMsg.message);
end
isVisuallyReasonable = ismember(polynomialDegrees,structResponse.isVisuallyReasonable);
workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit = workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit & isVisuallyReasonable;
observation = "Remaining degrees: " + jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit)) + newline + "Rationale: " + wrapText(structResponse.rationale);
end
%[text] Create an `openAIFunction` object that represents the `visuallyInspectPlotsOfPolynomialFits` function. To help the agent understand the purpose of the tool, provide a description.
tools(end+1) = aisdk.LLMTool(@visuallyInspectPlotsOfPolynomialFits, ...
    Description="Given a list of polynomial degrees, generate plots of the data beyond the data range. Inspect the generated plots using a vision model. " + ...
        "Discard visually unreasonable fits, return remaining list of degrees.", ...
    Workspace="agent");
%%
%[text] ### Assess Goodness-of-Fit Statistics
%[text] The `checkGoodnessOfFit` function evaluates the quality of each polynomial fit using goodness-of-fit statistics:
%[text] - `adjrsquare` — Degree-of-freedom adjusted coefficient of determination.
%[text] - `sse` — Sum-of-squares error. \
%[text] First, the function rejects fits with a small `adjrsquare` or a very large `sse`, which both indicate underfitting.
%[text] Second, the function rejects fits that do not show significant improvement in either `adjrsquare` or `sse` compared to fits with lower-degree polynomials.
function [observation, workspace] = checkGoodnessOfFit(workspace)
polynomialDegrees = workspace.polynomials.Degree;

[sse,adjustedRSquare] = extractGOFFromData(workspace);
[isPoorFit,logPoorFit] = identifyPoorFits(polynomialDegrees,sse,adjustedRSquare); 
[isNotImproving,logNoImprovement] = identifyNotImproving(polynomialDegrees,sse,adjustedRSquare);

workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit = workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit & ~isPoorFit & ~isNotImproving;
observation = "Remaining degrees: " + jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit)) + newline + "Log: " + ...
    newline + logPoorFit + newline + logNoImprovement;
end
%[text] Create an `openAIFunction` object that represents the `checkGoodnessOfFit` function. To help the agent understand the purpose of the tool, provide a description.
tools(end+1) = aisdk.LLMTool(@checkGoodnessOfFit, ...
    Description="Given a list of polynomial degrees, check their goodness-of-fit statistics, namely SSE and Adjusted R-squared, to "+...
    "discard unreasonable fits.", ...
    Workspace="agent");
%%
%[text] ## Use Agent to Find Best Fit
%[text] Set up agent.
systemPrompt = "Use MATLAB functions to solve mathematical problems. " + ...
    "Use the tools provided. " + ...
    "You are a helpful assistant. Follow the user instructions carefully. " + ...
    "Do not use Markdown. ";

% Choose OpenAI or Ollama. Remember to change this in
% visuallyInspectPlotsOfPolynomialFits as well.
if true %[control:checkbox:44b3]{"position":[4,8]}
    client = aisdk.LLMClient("openai", "gpt-4.1-mini");
else
    client = aisdk.LLMClient("ollama", "qwen3");
end
agent = aisdk.AIAgent(client, SystemPrompt=systemPrompt, ...
    Tools=tools, ...
    Workspace=workspace, ...
    DisplayMode="detailed"); %[control:dropdown:758e]{"position":[17,27]}
%[text] Call agent:
userPrompt = "Fit a polynomial to the data. Try up to ninth degree, then choose the best one using the tools provided.";
answer = agent.run(userPrompt) %[output:2d367020] %[output:31853c37]
agent.Workspace.polynomials %[output:584988e8]
%%
%[text] The rest of this example demonstrates how the agent outputs and workspace can be used for further analysis, and implements some helper functions that were used by the tools fed into the agent.
%%
%[text] ## Plot Fit
%[text] View the fitted model that the agent returned.
% bestDegreeIndex = find(agent.Workspace.polynomials.Degree == bestDegree);
fittedModel = agent.Workspace.polynomials.FittedModel{agent.Workspace.polynomials.IsCandidateFit} %[output:78acb1d4]
%[text] Plot the polynomial fit returned by the agent.
xExtrapolated = linspace(max(agent.Workspace.x),max(agent.Workspace.x)+range(agent.Workspace.x)/10,10);
populationExtrapolated = fittedModel(xExtrapolated);
confidenceInterval = predint(fittedModel,xExtrapolated,0.95,'observation');

figure %[output:8f715c46]
plot(agent.Workspace.x,agent.Workspace.y,'*') %[output:8f715c46]
xlim([min(agent.Workspace.x),1.3*max(agent.Workspace.x)]) %[output:8f715c46]
hold on %[output:8f715c46]
fig = plot(fittedModel); %[output:8f715c46]
set(fig,'linewidth',0.5) %[output:8f715c46]
h = errorbar(xExtrapolated,populationExtrapolated,populationExtrapolated-confidenceInterval(:,1),confidenceInterval(:,2)-populationExtrapolated,'.'); %[output:8f715c46]
hold off %[output:8f715c46]
legend("Data","Fit","Extrapolation",Location='northwest'); %[output:8f715c46]
%%
%[text] ## Helper Functions
%[text] ### Create and Save Plot of Extrapolated Fit
%[text] Create i plot of the data and the polynomial fits of a given degree. Save the plot to the directory `tempDirectory`.
function imgName = createAndSaveExtrapolatedFitPlot(workspace,degree,tempDirectory)
fittedModel = workspace.polynomials.FittedModel{degree};
f = figure(Visible="off");
plot(workspace.x,workspace.y,'o');
% Extrapolate beyond the range of the data
dx = max(workspace.x) - min(workspace.x);
xlim([min(workspace.x)-dx,max(workspace.x)]+dx);
hold on
plot(fittedModel);
hold off
imgName = fullfile(tempDirectory,"plot" + degree + ".png");
saveas(f,imgName);
close(f)
end
%[text] ### Extract Goodness-of-Fit Statistics From Workspace Structure
function [sse,adjustedRSquare] = extractGOFFromData(workspace)
polynomialDegrees = workspace.polynomials.Degree;
sse = zeros(size(polynomialDegrees));
adjustedRSquare = zeros(size(polynomialDegrees));
for i = 1:length(polynomialDegrees)
    gof = workspace.polynomials.GOF{i};
    sse(i) = gof.sse;
    adjustedRSquare(i) = gof.adjrsquare;
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Identify Underfitted Polynomials Based on Goodness-of-Fit Statistics
function [isPoorFit,logPoorFit] = identifyPoorFits(polynomialDegrees,sse,adjustedRSquare)
thresholdAdjustedRSquare = max(adjustedRSquare) - 0.05;
thresholdSSE = 10 * min(sse);

isAdjustedRSquareTooLow = adjustedRSquare < thresholdAdjustedRSquare;
isSSETooLarge = sse > thresholdSSE;

isPoorFit = isAdjustedRSquareTooLow | isSSETooLarge;

log1 = sprintf("Step 1: Reject degrees %s due to low adjusted R2.", ...
    jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(isAdjustedRSquareTooLow)));

log2 = sprintf("Step 2: Reject degrees %s due to very high SSE.", ...
    jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(isSSETooLarge)));
logPoorFit = log1 + newline + log2;
end
%%
%[text] ### Identify Higher-Order Polynomials Without Improved Fit Based on Goodness-of-Fit Statistics
function [isNotImproving,logNotImproving] = identifyNotImproving(polynomialDegrees,sse,adjustedRSquare)
targetAdjustedRSquare = 0.001;
targetSSERelativeDecrease = 0.01;

actualAdjustedRSquareIncrease = diff(adjustedRSquare);
actualSSERelativeDecrease = -diff(sse) ./ sse(1:end-1);

adjustedRSquareDoesNotIncrease = actualAdjustedRSquareIncrease < 0;
actualFitDoesNotMeetTarget = (actualAdjustedRSquareIncrease < targetAdjustedRSquare & actualSSERelativeDecrease < targetSSERelativeDecrease);

isNotImproving = false(size(polynomialDegrees));
isNotImproving(2:end) = adjustedRSquareDoesNotIncrease | actualFitDoesNotMeetTarget; % Skip index 1 due to "diff" shortening the arrays

logNotImproving = sprintf("Step 3: Reject degrees %s as higher-order terms failed to improve adjR2 or SSE.", ...
    jsonencode(polynomialDegrees(isNotImproving)));
end
%%
%[text] ### Wrap Text
function wrappedText = wrapText(text)
    lines = textwrap(text,90);
    wrappedText = string(join(lines,newline));
end
